---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE_NAME } from '../config.js';
import { rates, appliances } from '../lib/data';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '../lib/seo';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { name: 'About', href: '/about/' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={`About ${SITE_NAME}`} description={`Why ${SITE_NAME} exists: honest, state-aware answers about what home electricity actually costs, computed from current EIA data.`} schema={[breadcrumbSchema(crumbs)]}>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-6 pb-10">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink-900 dark:text-ink-100">About {SITE_NAME}</h1>
    <div class="mt-6 prose-custom">
      <p>
        {SITE_NAME} answers one deceptively simple question — <em>"how much does this cost to run?"</em> —
        correctly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most answers on the internet are wrong for a boring reason: they assume a single national electricity
        price, often one from years ago. Real U.S. residential rates currently span from about
        {[...rates.states].sort((a, b) => a.cents - b.cents)[0].cents}¢ to
        {[...rates.states].sort((a, b) => b.cents - a.cents)[0].cents}¢ per kilowatt-hour — a spread wide enough
        that the same space heater can cost $40 or $115 a month depending on where it's plugged in. An answer
        that ignores your state isn't an estimate; it's a coin flip.
      </p>
      <p>
        So we built the version we wanted: {appliances.length} appliances with realistic wattage and usage data,
        priced with <strong>current, state-level rates</strong> refreshed monthly from U.S. Energy Information
        Administration data, with calculators on every page so you can swap in your own habits. No account, no
        app, no upsell — the site is free, supported by advertising.
      </p>
      <h2>What we care about</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The answer first.</strong> Every page leads with the number you came for, then shows the assumptions behind it.</li>
        <li><strong>Current data.</strong> Rates carry their vintage ({rates.periodLabel} right now) and update on a schedule, not "whenever."</li>
        <li><strong>Honest magnitudes.</strong> We'll tell you when something costs pennies and isn't worth optimizing — unplugging your toaster is not a savings plan.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        How every figure is computed, including sources and formulas, is documented on the
        <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>. Corrections and suggestions are genuinely welcome via the
        <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
